1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,062
Why is this lake in Australia as

2
00:00:02,062 --> 00:00:03,221
pink as bubblegum?

3
00:00:03,571 --> 00:00:06,151
This is Lake Hillier, on Middle Island,

4
00:00:06,151 --> 00:00:08,862
off the south coast of Western Australia.

5
00:00:09,212 --> 00:00:11,856
It's only about six hundred meters long,

6
00:00:11,856 --> 00:00:13,706
and a thin strip of sand and

7
00:00:13,706 --> 00:00:16,218
forest separates it from the deep blue

8
00:00:16,218 --> 00:00:16,615
ocean.

9
00:00:16,965 --> 00:00:19,419
The pink color is believed to come

10
00:00:19,419 --> 00:00:22,451
from a tiny alga called Dunaliella salina.

11
00:00:22,451 --> 00:00:24,977
It makes red pigments to survive in

12
00:00:24,977 --> 00:00:28,298
very salty water, and salt-loving bacteria add

13
00:00:28,298 --> 00:00:29,381
even more pink.

14
00:00:29,731 --> 00:00:31,498
The lake is about as salty as

15
00:00:31,498 --> 00:00:32,291
the Dead Sea.

16
00:00:32,641 --> 00:00:35,384
The first written record of it came

17
00:00:35,384 --> 00:00:38,677
in 1802, from the British explorer Matthew

18
00:00:38,677 --> 00:00:39,382
Flinders.

19
00:00:39,732 --> 00:00:42,471
And surprisingly, the water is safe to

20
00:00:42,471 --> 00:00:44,923
swim in. But the island is remote,

21
00:00:44,923 --> 00:00:47,157
so most people only ever see it

22
00:00:47,157 --> 00:00:48,095
from a plane.

23
00:00:48,445 --> 00:00:50,853
It's not the only one. Australia has

24
00:00:50,853 --> 00:00:53,329
other pink lakes, like Hutt Lagoon on

25
00:00:53,329 --> 00:00:54,333
the west coast.

26
00:00:54,683 --> 00:00:56,377
Would you swim in a pink lake?

27
00:00:56,377 --> 00:00:57,733
Tell me in the comments.

